\par Default Constructor: When constructors do not have parameters, then it is called the default constructor. These types of constructors have all its instance initialized with the same value.
\par 
\par Parameterized Constructor: When any constructor has at least one parameter, it is called the parameterized constructor.
\par 
\par Copy Constructor: When the constructor is used to create an object just by copying all of its variables from another object, such constructors are called copy constructor. They are used for initializing a new instance from an existing one.
\par 
\par ex
\par 
\par public class MachinePart
\par \{
\par     public string Name \{ get; set; \}        // \{ get; set; \}  This is the "magic" part that makes them Auto-Properties:
\par 
\par get: Allows other parts of your code to read the value. Example: string myName = myPart.Name; set: Allows other parts of your code to change the value. Example: myPart.Weight = 2.5;
\par 
\par     // Standard constructor
\par     public MachinePart(string name, double weight)
\par     \{
\par         Name = name;
\par         Weight = weight;
\par     \}
\par 
\par     // The Copy Constructor
\par     public MachinePart(MachinePart other)
\par     \{
\par         // Copying values from the 'other' object to the new instance
\par         this.Name = other.Name;
\par         this.Weight = other.Weight;
\par     \}
\par \}
\par 
\par // Usage:
\par MachinePart originalPart = new MachinePart("Laser Nozzle", 0.5);
\par 
\par // Creating a new instance using the Copy Constructor
\par MachinePart clonedPart = new MachinePart(originalPart);
\par 
\par // Now clonedPart has the same values as originalPart
\par 
\par Private Constructor: When a constructor is produced with a private access modifier, it is called Private Constructor. It does not make it possible for other classes to inherit any data from this class.
\par 
\par Static Constructor: When a constructor needs to be invoked only once, and when that constructor needs to be invoked at creating the first reference, then those constructors are made static and are called static constructors.
\par 
